  "preamble": "Proto-Semitic had a voiced velar or uvular fricative *ġ [ɣ~ʁ] distinct from the pharyngeal *ʿ [ʕ]. Arabic, Ugaritic and the Old South Arabian script kept them apart, but Canaanite (including Hebrew), Aramaic and Geʽez merged *ġ into ʿayin. Greek transcriptions such as Gaza and Gomorrha show that Hebrew still had the velar or uvular sound in the 3rd century BCE; the merger was complete by Tiberian Hebrew. Among the Modern South Arabian languages, Soqotri merged native /ɣ/ with /ʕ/ in its Central-Eastern dialects, while Mehri and Hobyot keep ġ.",
